            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Unfortunately, it is not an easy thing to do. The reason is because in order to make this work with \"next\"/\"prev\" search methods, 'mini.ai' needs to know about them when deciding which one to choose. The built-in commenting was designed to exclusively used interactively, so it doesn't provide any (stable) Lua API.\r\n\r\nThe best approach to solve what you are asking for is to manually write a textobject specification as a [function that returns an array of regions](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-ai.txt#L294-L313) representing all consecutive comment blocks. Depending on how robust and capable you want it to be, implementation can span from maybe about 15 lines to at least 50.\r\n\r\nHowever, the most reasonable approach to solve the problem I'd say is to utilize [`'mini.bracketed' `comment` target](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L38) to move across comment blocks and using available \"current comment block\" `gc` textobject.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Unfortunately, it is not an easy thing to do. The reason is because in order to make this work with \"next\"/\"prev\" search methods, 'mini.ai' needs to know about them when deciding which one to choose. The built-in commenting was designed to exclusively used interactively, so it doesn't provide any (stable) Lua API.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The best approach to solve what you are asking for is to manually write a textobject specification as a <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-ai.txt#L294-L313\">function that returns an array of regions</a> representing all consecutive comment blocks. Depending on how robust and capable you want it to be, implementation can span from maybe about 15 lines to at least 50.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">However, the most reasonable approach to solve the problem I'd say is to utilize [<code class=\"notranslate\">'mini.bracketed' </code>comment<code class=\"notranslate\">target](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L38) to move across comment blocks and using available \"current comment block\"</code>gc` textobject.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Unfortunately, it is not an easy thing to do. The reason is because in order to make this work with \"next\"/\"prev\" search methods, 'mini.ai' needs to know about them when deciding which one to choose. The built-in commenting was designed to exclusively used interactively, so it doesn't provide any (stable) Lua API.\nThe best approach to solve what you are asking for is to manually write a textobject specification as a function that returns an array of regions representing all consecutive comment blocks. Depending on how robust and capable you want it to be, implementation can span from maybe about 15 lines to at least 50.\nHowever, the most reasonable approach to solve the problem I'd say is to utilize ['mini.bracketed' commenttarget](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L38) to move across comment blocks and using available \"current comment block\"gc` textobject.",
